{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Evolution has no goal of 'truth,' whereas Popperian science explicitly aims at verisimilitude.","A courtroom verdict often carries a presumption of finality or probability ('beyond reasonable doubt'), whereas Popperian corroboration explicitly denies any increase in probability."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'survivor' status is shown to be purely accidental relative to the test conditions (luck rather than resilience).","If the goal of the system is redefined from 'truth-seeking' to 'prediction-accuracy,' rendering the non-probabilistic stance irrational."],"shared_structure":["Survival of a candidate does not prove its intrinsic superiority, only its resilience against specific challenges.","Preference is given to the survivor not because it is guaranteed to be true/right, but because it is the only one remaining standing after rigorous attempts to eliminate it.","The process is eliminative rather than confirmatory."],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_selection_process","courtroom_adversarial_system"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a biologist prefers the fittest organism for study without claiming it is the 'perfect' species, a scientist prefers the best-corroborated theory without claiming it is probable.","The rationality of the choice lies in the rigor of the elimination process, not in the attributes of the survivor."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive methodological rule aimed at maximizing verisimilitude (truth-likeness) through severe testing, but accepting this defense costs the scientist any claim to predictive certainty or probabilistic confidence, reducing rational action to a bold conjecture without inductive backing.","counterconditions":["If 'severity of test' implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature (that future conditions will resemble past testing conditions), then the preference smuggles in induction.","If the concept of 'verisimilitude' cannot be formally defined or assessed without inductive inference, then the rationale collapses into the very probabilistic thinking Popper rejected.","If the methodological rule is merely an arbitrary convention without rational force, it fails to explain why scientists *should* prefer corroborated theories rather than just noting that they *do*."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument proceeds by distinguishing between 'justification' (which implies induction) and 'critical preference' (which relies on the logical asymmetry of falsification). It posits that choosing the theory with the highest content that has survived the most severe tests is the only rational strategy if one's goal is to get closer to the truth, even if one cannot know that one has succeeded. The mechanism rejects the idea that past survival guarantees future success, instead framing the choice as the best available bet in a game where the odds are unknown but the potential payoff (truth) is maximized by high content and rigorous screening.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}